  "commit_id": "f9c6c74bdcdd43eed73a95dca7cb7219edd4eed5",
  "event": "REQUEST_CHANGES",
  "body": "Requesting changes here before this can merge. Three issues below need to be addressed, and I've left more detailed feedback inline for a few other items worth a look.\n\n**1. When every colour is sold out, the page shows the wrong thing.** Left a comment on the exact line, but here's what's happening: there's a flag called `suppressColourAttribute` that's supposed to turn off the old colour picker whenever your new one takes over. But when every colour is unavailable, that flag flips back to false, so the old picker turns back on, and it shows every unavailable colour as a greyed out square instead of just showing nothing. There's no product in the current test catalogue where every colour is actually unavailable, so I couldn't click through this live. I wrote a quick script instead, using the real logic from both files, to run it against a made up product with everything unavailable and confirm the mechanism directly. Happy to share it if useful.\n\n**2. The new Playwright tests don't actually run.** The test file calls five functions to get test products (`pdpProductWithMultipleColours` and four others) that don't exist anywhere in the fixtures file, and it also expects around ten things on the `ProductPage` helper that were never added. I ran the tests against a real dev server just to check: all 11 failed, none of them skipped. They're written well otherwise, once the missing pieces exist they should actually catch real bugs, but I'm not sure what the value is in including 11 tests in a PR when none of them can currently run or be verified. Might be worth updating the PR description too, since right now it says all 11 are skipping because of missing test data, and that's not what's happening.\n\n**3. Colour swatches turned into squares everywhere, not just on this page.** There's a line in `product-view` that used to pick circle or square depending on the attribute, and now it's just always square, and it's not wrapped in the same check that turns off the old picker. So cart, wishlist, and bonus product all get square swatches now too, not just this PDP. I checked the cart's edit modal and yeah, it's square there now. This styles things well outside the scope of the colour swatch on this PDP, and it's actually wrong for those other places too, the expanded gallery for example uses rounded colour swatches by design, so this breaks that. Changes here should stay scoped to just this page's colour swatch, not touch the shared component's default behaviour.",
  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/pages/product-detail/index.tsx",
      "line": 905,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "This is the actual line behind the first issue above. `hasOfferedColour` comes back false in two different situations: when there's no colour option at all, and when there is one but every value got filtered out. Only the second case is the problem, since it also turns off the suppression and lets the old picker show through."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/index.tsx",
      "line": 838,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Same thing as the general comment. This used to switch between circle and square depending on which attribute it was, and now it's just always square. This looks like it changed without realizing `product-view` is shared across cart, wishlist, and bonus product too, since none of those pass the new suppress flag, so they're all getting square swatches now as a side effect. Worth checking where else a component is used before changing something this central to it. This should go back to picking circle or square based on the attribute like before, so it only affects this PDP."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/utils/filterImageGroups.ts",
      "line": 4,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Quick one: the comment says this doesn't exist as a public export elsewhere, but it actually does, it's exported from `product-utils.js`, and this PR already imports two other things from that same file (`getPriceData` and `getUpdateBundleChildArray`). Might be simpler to just use the existing one instead of keeping a second copy around, especially since they don't behave quite the same (the original one handles a missing `imageGroups` fine, this one throws), which is an easy thing to lose track of later."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/colourSwatches/colourSwatches.css.ts",
      "line": 61,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "The disabled chevron here just fades out with `opacity: 0.3`. I checked the same element on the other version of this feature against Figma directly, and the spec actually wants a colour change, not a fade. This goes against how disabled states are handled everywhere else in this design system, which is always through colour tokens, not opacity, so this needs to switch to the colour based approach to stay consistent."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/colourSwatches/colourSwatches.css.ts",
      "line": 103,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Looked up the real Figma dimensions for this swatch, and the actual image is meant to be a bit taller relative to its width than what's here, sitting in the middle of the box with a little space above and below rather than filling the whole thing edge to edge."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/colourSwatches/index.tsx",
      "line": 79,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Quick question, do you have a link for where this was decided? I looked through Jira, Confluence, and the Figma file for this story and couldn't find it anywhere, and the design itself doesn't show a disabled look for colour swatches at all."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/colourSwatches/index.test.tsx",
      "line": 29,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "None of the test fixtures here go past 3 colours, so the part that kicks in at 4+ (the pagination) never actually gets tested. Also saw a comment saying Storybook's interaction tests fail across the whole repo right now, which is why pagination wasn't covered that way instead, but I ran your `SevenColours` story locally just now and the pagination test in it passed fine. Might be worth adding that coverage here."
    },
    {
      "path": "translations/en-GB.json",
      "line": 966,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "This adds a translation key for the header search button, but this PR doesn't touch the header at all. Guessing this came in from another branch by accident. Worth double checking the full diff for anything unrelated before opening a PR, just to keep things scoped to what the ticket actually covers."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/colourSwatches/index.tsx",
      "line": 69,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Small lint warning that showed up comparing this branch to develop, an unbound method reference here. Easy fix, just flagging in case it got missed."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/pages/product-detail/partials/staticProductHeader.test.tsx",
      "line": 42,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "Same lint sweep, an unnecessary type assertion here. Easy fix, just flagging in case it got missed."
    },
    {
      "path": "overrides/app/components/product-view/colourSwatches/colourSwatches.css.ts",
      "line": 84,
      "side": "RIGHT",
      "body": "This shows the focus ring on `:focus` instead of `:focus-visible`, with a comment saying keyboard focus from arrow keys doesn't always trigger `:focus-visible` properly. That's actually only true for one of the two spots where focus gets set. When you arrow between swatches on the same page, focus is set right there in the keydown handler, and that works fine with `:focus-visible`, browsers reliably treat a `.focus()` call made synchronously inside a keydown handler as keyboard driven. It's only when arrowing across a page change, where the focus happens a moment later after the page updates, that the browser can lose track of it being a keyboard action, and that specific case is the one that actually needs its own fix.\n\nRight now, because it's just `:focus`, the ring shows up on a regular mouse click too. I tried it, click a swatch and the ring appears, click somewhere else and it goes away. That's normal for `:focus`, but it defeats the entire point of using `:focus-visible`, and this should switch back to it rather than staying on `:focus`. This is also the kind of thing that's easy to catch just by clicking around before pushing, worth a quick visual pass on interactive states like this before opening a PR."
    }
  ]
}

